  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cape Verde - Support to the Setting up of the National Investment System (NIS)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&quot;Cape Verde&#x2019;s growth strategies have recognized the role of infrastructure development in fostering key economic sectors. The government&#x2019;s First, Second and Third Growth and Poverty Reduction Strategy Papers (GPRSP I-III), covering the periods 2003-07, 2008-11 and 2012-15 respectively, clearly define the development of infrastructure as a necessary condition for improving the conditions for economic growth and poverty reduction. The Government has taken significant steps to close its infrastructure deficits through high public investment rates which have largely been financed by an intense external revenue mobilization effort. In the last decades public investment in Cape Verde averaged 15 percent of GDP, a rate that is much higher than the levels observed in Sub-Saharan African (SSA) countries. Currently, further gains can be made in Cape Verde by shifting the focus from accelerated capital accumulation to improvements in the quality and efficiency of public infrastructure.  In this regard, there are gains to be made by improving the quality and efficiency of Cape Verde&#x2019;s public invetsment management.  Improvements in public investment management (PIM) system are integral part of the World Bank dialogue with the government.In FY2, the Bank provided a NLTA project on the CV PIM system that prepared a diagnosis and an action plan to improve it. In addition, in FY11-12, the Luxemburg Trust Fund on capacity building for the Government wide M&amp;E system included training courses on Appraisal Techniques for Public Investment projects.  The forthcoming Country Partnership Strategy (CPS) for FY13-16, which is expected to be approved by the Board in Q3 FY13 will contain support on this area. This activity aims at implementing the action plan that was prepared under the NLTA activity. . The action plan identifies four areas to focus on strengthening the PIM in Cape Verde:  &#x2022;	Capacity building for strategic planning; &#x2022;	Providing technical guidance for ex ante and ex post evaluations; &#x2022;	Implementation and monitoring of assets; &#x2022;	Training of staff in line ministries on project appraisal techniques.&quot;</narrative>
  </description>
